WN.lb(42,{"mt_9ZLmSrRZmH":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Who is the calf's mummy?","choices":["A cow","A cat","A hen"],"answer":0,"hint":"A calf is a baby. It drinks milk from a big farm animal.","explain":"A calf is a baby cow. Its mummy is a cow."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"{n} puppies are playing. What will they grow into?","choices":["Dogs","Cats","They stay puppies"],"answer":0,"hint":"A puppy is a baby. Which grown-up is it a baby of?","explain":"Puppies are baby dogs, so all {n} puppies grow into dogs.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A sheep and her lamb. Which one is the baby?","choices":["The small lamb","The big sheep","They are both babies"],"answer":0,"hint":"Babies are small. Grown-ups are big.","explain":"The small lamb is the baby. The big sheep is its mummy."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A duckling grows up. What is it now?","choices":["A duck","A hen","A bigger duckling"],"answer":0,"hint":"A duckling is a baby. Who is its mummy?","explain":"A duckling is a baby duck, so it grows up into a duck, not a hen."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What is a baby horse called?","choices":["A foal","A piglet","A chick"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is the one we saw with the horse in the lab.","explain":"A baby horse is a foal. A piglet is a baby pig and a chick is a baby chicken."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"There are {n} piglets in the mud. Who is their mummy?","choices":["A pig","A dog","A sheep"],"answer":0,"hint":"Piglet sounds like which animal?","explain":"Piglets are baby pigs, so the mummy of all {n} piglets is a pig.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What is a baby cat called?","choices":["A kitten","A puppy","A cat"],"answer":0,"hint":"It has its own name, not cat.","explain":"A baby cat is a kitten. A puppy is a baby dog."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A chick hatches from an egg. Who is its mummy?","choices":["A hen","A duck","A cow"],"answer":0,"hint":"A chick is a baby. Which bird says cluck?","explain":"A chick is a baby chicken, and its mummy is a hen. A duck's baby is a duckling."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Who is the lamb's mummy?","choices":["A sheep","A cow","A duck"],"answer":0,"hint":"A lamb is a baby. Which big animal is woolly like it?","explain":"A lamb is a baby sheep, so its mummy is a sheep."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A cat has {n} babies. What are they called?","choices":["Kittens","Puppies","Calves"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the name for a baby cat.","explain":"Baby cats are kittens, so the cat has {n} kittens. Puppies are baby dogs.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A calf grows and grows. What will it be?","choices":["A cow","A horse","A bigger calf for ever"],"answer":0,"hint":"A calf is a baby. Who is its mummy?","explain":"A calf is a baby cow, so it grows into a cow. Babies do not stay small."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A hen sits on {n} eggs. What comes out?","choices":["Chicks","Ducklings","Lambs"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is a baby chicken called?","explain":"Baby chickens are chicks, so {n} chicks hatch. Ducklings come from duck eggs.","params":{"n":[3,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Animals have babies. A baby is small. Its mummy is big.","A lamb is a baby sheep. A calf is a baby cow. A kitten is a baby cat. A duckling is a baby duck.","A puppy is a baby dog. A chick is a baby chicken. A piglet is a baby pig. A foal is a baby horse.","Babies grow and grow. A kitten grows into a cat. It does not turn into a dog!"],"adult":["This topic is about matching common farm and pet animals with their young, learning the special names for the babies, and knowing that a baby grows up into the same kind of animal as its parent. It builds the early idea of life cycles and offspring that later science work depends on.","In the lab your child walks four babies to their mothers, names the baby of a dog, sheep, pig and horse, taps to grow a kitten into a cat and a chick into a hen, sorts six animals into babies and grown-ups, and finds a lamb, chick and calf and then their mothers on a farm page.","Common mistakes: using one baby word for every animal (calling a lamb a puppy); mixing up the chick and the duckling, which both hatch as small fluffy birds; thinking a baby stays small, or grows into a different animal; and judging only by size, so a big lamb next to a small breed of sheep can confuse. Say both words together: a lamb is a baby sheep.","Picture books, farm visits and toy animals all help. Hold up a toy sheep and ask, \"Where is her baby? What is it called?\" Then swap roles and let your child ask you."]},"familyTip":"Next time you read a farm book or see animals out and about, play 'find the baby': spot a young animal, say its name, then find its mummy. Start with lamb and sheep or calf and cow, and add one new name each week.","keyIdea":"Animals have babies. A baby is a small one of the same kind of animal, and it grows into a grown-up like its mummy. Many babies have their own names: a lamb is a baby sheep, a calf is a baby cow, a kitten is a baby cat and a duckling is a baby duck.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Whose baby is it? Find its mummy. Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🐑 Sheep","🐄 Cow","🐔 Hen"],"items":[["lamb",0],["baby sheep",0],["calf",1],["baby cow",1],["chick",2],["baby chicken",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbaby","caption":"Flagship lab: take babies home to their mummies, name the babies, grow a kitten and a chick, sort big and small, and find babies on a farm page.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_v19S17T_LF":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which one is NOT alive?","choices":["A stone","A tree","A fish"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two of these eat or drink and grow. One never does.","explain":"A stone is not alive. A tree drinks water and grows, and a fish eats and grows, so they are alive."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How do we know a flower is alive?","choices":["It drinks water and grows","It is pretty","It has a nice smell"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the clues: eat or drink, and grow.","explain":"A flower drinks water and grows, so it is alive. Being pretty or smelling nice does not tell you."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ben says, \"A teddy bear is alive. It has eyes and a mouth.\" Is he right?","choices":["No, a teddy cannot eat or grow","Yes, it has a face","Yes, because it is soft"],"answer":0,"hint":"Looks do not tell you. Can the teddy eat?","explain":"Ben is not right. A teddy looks like a bear, but it cannot eat, drink or grow, so it is not alive."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A worm wriggles {n} centimetres to a leaf all by itself and eats it. Is the worm alive?","choices":["Yes, it moves by itself and eats","No, it is too small","No, it lives in mud"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did anyone push the worm? Did it eat?","explain":"The worm is alive. It moves by itself, it eats, and it grows.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You see a snail, a rock, a bucket and a bush in the garden. Which two are alive?","choices":["The snail and the bush","The rock and the bucket","The bucket and the snail"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which ones eat or drink, and grow?","explain":"The snail and the bush are alive. They eat or drink, and grow. A rock and a bucket never grow."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A toy car rolls {n} steps down a slope. Then it stops. Is the car alive?","choices":["No, the slope made it roll","Yes, it moved","Yes, until it stopped"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did the car move by itself, or did the slope do it?","explain":"The toy car is not alive. The slope made it roll. It cannot eat, drink or grow.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What does a living thing need?","choices":["Food or water","A battery","Paint"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do you give a plant or a pet each day?","explain":"Living things need food or water. A battery runs toys, and toys are not alive."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A tree stands in the park. It never walks anywhere. Is it alive?","choices":["Yes, it drinks and grows","No, it never moves","No, it is made of wood"],"answer":0,"hint":"Plants do not walk. Do they grow?","explain":"The tree is alive. It drinks water through its roots and grows taller each year."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which one is alive?","choices":["A bird","A spoon","A toy car"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one eats food and grows bigger?","explain":"A bird is alive. It eats, drinks and grows. A spoon and a toy car cannot do any of those."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Sam plants {n} seeds. He gives them water. Days later, they have grown. Are the seeds alive?","choices":["Yes, they drink and grow","No, they cannot walk","No, they are too small"],"answer":0,"hint":"Plants do not walk. What else do living things do?","explain":"Yes. Seeds and plants are alive. They drink water and grow, even though they stay in one place.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A toy robot walks across the floor. Is it alive?","choices":["No, a battery makes it go","Yes, it moves","Yes, it has eyes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can the robot eat? Can it grow?","explain":"The robot is not alive. It moves only when a battery runs it, and it can never eat or grow."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Mia gives food to a puppy and to a teddy. After {n} weeks, which one has grown?","choices":["The puppy","The teddy","Both of them"],"answer":0,"hint":"Only one of them can eat the food. Which one?","explain":"The puppy has grown. It is alive, so it eats and grows. The teddy cannot eat, and it stays the same size.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Grandpa's cat is fast asleep on the rug. It does not move. Is it alive?","choices":["Yes, it is alive","No, it is not moving","Only when it wakes up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the cat still breathe, eat and grow?","explain":"Yes, the cat is alive while it sleeps. Being still does not make something not alive."}],"explain":{"kid":["A dog is alive. It eats. It drinks. It grows big.","A rock is not alive. It does not eat. It does not grow.","A plant is alive too! It drinks water and it grows.","A toy robot can walk. But it is not alive. It cannot eat. It cannot grow. It stops when the battery runs out."],"adult":["This topic, for ages 3 to 4, is about sorting things into living (people, animals, plants) and not living (rocks, toys, cars) and giving one simple reason. It is the first step towards the life processes children meet later, such as nutrition, growth, movement and reproduction.","Give your child three easy clues: living things eat or drink, they grow, and they move or change by themselves. A worm eats leaves and wriggles on its own. A seed grows into a plant. A toy car only moves when someone pushes it or a battery runs it, and it never grows.","Common mistakes at this age: thinking anything that moves is alive (cars, clouds, a wind-up toy), thinking plants are not alive because they do not walk, thinking a teddy is alive because it has a face, and thinking a sleeping animal is not alive because it is still. Ask \"Does it eat or drink? Does it grow?\" rather than \"Does it move?\"","In the lab your child sorts things into two boxes, watches a seed and a puppy grow while a car and a stone stay the same, feeds a cat, a plant and a teddy, switches on a robot that stops when its battery is flat next to a worm that wriggles by itself, and then sorts the tricky ones."]},"familyTip":"On a walk, play \"Alive or not?\" Point at a bird, a bench, a tree, a car and a snail. Ask \"Does it eat or drink? Does it grow?\" before your child decides. Plant a bean in a cup and check it each morning.","keyIdea":"Living things eat or drink, grow, and move or change by themselves. People, animals and plants are alive. Rocks, toys and cars are not. Moving is not enough: a toy robot moves, but it cannot eat or grow. Staying still does not mean dead: a tree and a sleeping cat are alive.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Alive or not alive? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Alive","Not alive"],"items":[["bird",0],["snail",0],["bush",0],["toy car",1],["bucket",1],["stone",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagalive","caption":"Flagship lab: sort things into alive and not alive, watch what grows, feed a cat, a plant and a teddy, test a robot and a worm, then sort the tricky ones.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_QrrRKNsKhk":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Where are your toes?","choices":["On your feet","On your hands","On your head"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look all the way down. What is at the end of your legs?","explain":"Toes are on your feet, at the very bottom. The parts on your hands are fingers."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Head, shoulders, knees and ...?","choices":["Toes","Nose","Elbows"],"answer":0,"hint":"The song goes down the body. What is at the very bottom?","explain":"Head, shoulders, knees and toes. The song goes from the top of you to the bottom."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Your arm bends in the middle. What is that called?","choices":["Elbow","Knee","Shoulder"],"answer":0,"hint":"Bend your arm and touch the pointy bit in the middle.","explain":"Your arm bends at the elbow. The knee is the bend in your leg."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What helps you eat your apple?","choices":["Your mouth","Your knees","Your toes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where does the apple go when you take a bite?","explain":"Your mouth helps you eat. You bite and chew with it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Gran opens her arms. She gives you {n} big hugs. What does she hug with?","choices":["Arms","Feet","Ears"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which parts wrap around you in a hug?","explain":"Gran hugs with her arms. They wrap around you all {n} times.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Where is your tummy?","choices":["In the middle","At the top","On your feet"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is between your head and your legs. Pat it!","explain":"Your tummy is in the middle of your body, between your head and your legs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Leo jumps {n} times on the spot. What helps him jump?","choices":["Legs and feet","His nose","His elbows"],"answer":0,"hint":"What pushes you up off the floor?","explain":"Legs and feet push Leo up off the floor for all {n} jumps. His knees bend to help.","params":{"n":[2,8]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Where is your head?","choices":["At the top","At the bottom","In the middle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Touch the very top of you. What part is up there?","explain":"Your head is at the top of your body. Your tummy is in the middle and your feet are at the bottom."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Kit takes {n} big steps across the room. Which body part helps Kit walk?","choices":["Legs","Ears","Hands"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which parts move when you walk across the room?","explain":"Legs help Kit walk. Each of the {n} steps is one leg moving forward, then the other.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Your leg bends in the middle. What is that part called?","choices":["Knee","Elbow","Toe"],"answer":0,"hint":"Your arm bends at the elbow. The leg has its own bend.","explain":"Your leg bends at the knee. Your arm bends at the elbow, so they are different bends."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Mia claps her hands {n} times. What does she clap with?","choices":["Hands","Feet","Knees"],"answer":0,"hint":"Clap is a noise you make by bringing two parts together. Which two?","explain":"Mia claps {n} times with her hands. Feet can stamp, but hands clap.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["This is your head. It is at the top. Your tummy is in the middle. Your toes are at the bottom, on your feet. Can you touch them?","Sing it! Head, shoulders, knees and toes. The song goes from the top down. Touch each part as you sing.","Your arm can bend. It bends at the elbow. Your leg can bend too. It bends at the knee. Bend, then make it straight!","Each part helps you. Legs help you walk and jump. Hands help you clap. Your mouth helps you eat. Arms give a big hug."],"adult":["At two and three, children learn to name and point to the main parts of the body: head, shoulders, tummy, arms, elbows, hands, legs, knees, feet and toes. They also start to say simple things each part does, such as legs for walking. This is early science vocabulary, and it also helps a child tell you where something hurts.","Toddlers learn body words best through touch and movement. Naming a part while they touch it, and songs such as Head, Shoulders, Knees and Toes, link the word to the place. Working from the top of the body to the bottom gives them a map: head at the top, tummy in the middle, feet at the bottom. Bending an elbow and a knee shows that these are the joints where arms and legs fold.","Common mistakes to watch for: mixing up elbow and knee, since both are bends; calling the whole leg a foot or the whole arm a hand; saying toes for fingers; and pointing to the chest for tummy. Correct by touching both parts and naming them side by side, for example: this is your elbow on your arm, this is your knee on your leg.","In the lab your child taps head, tummy, hands and toes on Kit, taps the song parts in order from top to bottom, drags a hand and a foot to bend the elbow and the knee, taps the part that helps Kit walk, clap, eat and hug, and builds Kit by putting the head on top, the arms at the sides and the legs at the bottom."]},"familyTip":"At bath time or while dressing, play Touch Your: say \"Touch your elbow!\" and then swap so your child gives the orders. Sing Head, Shoulders, Knees and Toes slowly, then faster. Ask, \"What do your legs help you do?\" and let your child show you.","keyIdea":"Your body has parts with names. Your head is at the top, your tummy is in the middle, and your toes are at the bottom on your feet. Arms bend at the elbow and legs bend at the knee. Each part helps you do things: legs help you walk, hands help you clap, your mouth helps you eat and arms give a hug.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Top half or bottom half of the body? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["⬆ Top half","⬇ Bottom half"],"items":[["Head",0],["Shoulders",0],["Elbows",0],["Nose",0],["Knees",1],["Toes",1],["Feet",1],["Ankles",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbody","caption":"Flagship lab: find body parts on Kit, tap the song in order, bend an elbow and a knee, find the part for each job, and build Kit.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Vw2uPbCosk":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A plant grew in a dark cupboard. What does it look like?","choices":["Tall, thin and pale","Green and strong","Covered in flowers"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does a plant need light for?","explain":"In the dark, a plant stretches up looking for light. It grows thin, and its leaves go pale."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Two plants: a tall pale one from the cupboard and a short green one from the window. Which is healthy?","choices":["The short green one","The tall pale one","Both are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is taller always better? Think about what the cupboard plant was missing.","explain":"The green one had light and is strong. The tall pale one is thin and floppy because it had no light."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A plant is next to a window. Which way does it lean?","choices":["Toward the window","Away from the window","It never leans"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where does the light come from?","explain":"Plants grow toward the light, so it bends to the window."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What does a plant need to stay healthy?","choices":["Water and light","Sweets and toys","Only water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the droopy plant and the pale plant.","explain":"A plant needs water and light, and to be warm enough. Water alone is not enough."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"{n} plants are on the windowsill. {k} have dry soil. How many need water?","answer":"k","hint":"Which plants need water: the dry ones or the wet ones?","explain":"Only the plants with dry soil need water, so {k} need water. The others can wait.","params":{"n":[5,6],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"How can you tell if a plant needs water?","choices":["Touch the soil: is it dry?","Look at the pot colour","Count the leaves"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where does the plant get its water from?","explain":"The roots drink from the soil. Dry soil means it is time to water."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"You water a plant on day 1. Its soil is dry again after {d} days. On which day do you water it next?","answer":"1+d","hint":"Start at day 1 and count on {d} days.","explain":"Day 1 and {d} more days is day {=1+d}. The soil is dry then, so that is when to water.","params":{"d":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A pale plant has wet soil and sits in a dark corner. What does it need?","choices":["Light","More water","A smaller pot"],"answer":0,"hint":"The soil is wet already. What else is missing?","explain":"The soil is wet, so water is not the problem. Pale leaves in the dark mean it needs light."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A plant on the windowsill droops. Its soil is dry. What does it need?","choices":["Water","A dark cupboard","A sweet"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the soil. What is missing?","explain":"Dry soil means the roots have no water to drink. Water it and the leaves stand up again."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Kit has {n} plants. She puts 1 in a dark cupboard and the rest on the windowsill. How many plants have light?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Take away the one in the cupboard.","explain":"{n} plants with 1 in the dark leaves {=n-1} plants in the light.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The soil is still wet. Do you water the plant again?","choices":["No, wait until it is dry","Yes, more is better","Yes, fill the pot to the top"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can roots get too much water?","explain":"Too much water can rot the roots. Wait until the soil feels dry, then water."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Your bean plant is {a} blocks tall. With water and light it grows {b} blocks more. How tall is it now?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Start at {a} and count on {b} more.","explain":"{a} blocks and {b} more blocks make {=a+b} blocks tall. Water and light helped it grow.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,3]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A plant drinks water from the soil. Touch the soil. Is it dry? Give it water. Is it wet? Wait.","No water? The plant droops. Its leaves hang down. Give it water and it stands up again.","A plant needs light too. Put a plant in a dark cupboard. It grows tall and thin. Its leaves go pale. It is not well.","A plant leans to the light. Put it by a window. It bends to the window."],"adult":["This is early plant biology. In the EYFS, Development Matters asks three and four year olds to plant seeds and care for growing plants, and England's Year 2 science asks children to find out and describe how plants need water, light and a suitable temperature to grow and stay healthy. US NGSS K-LS1-1 asks children to describe what plants need to live, using what they see.","Roots take up water from the soil; without it the plant wilts because its cells lose the water that keeps them firm. Leaves use light to make the plant's food (photosynthesis), so a plant grown in the dark stretches into tall, thin, pale shoots, which botanists call etiolation. A plant bends toward light from one side, called phototropism.","Common mistakes: thinking more water is always better (waterlogged soil starves roots of air and they can rot), thinking the tallest plant is the healthiest, thinking a plant in the dark will not grow at all, and thinking plants eat food from the soil the way we eat. A common adult slip is watering on a fixed schedule instead of checking whether the soil is dry first.","In the lab your child waters a pot plant over five days, but only when the soil is dry. Next, they read the clue on a sad plant and give it water or light. They grow two seedlings, one in a dark cupboard, and compare them. They move a lamp and watch the plant lean to it. Last, they sort what a plant needs."]},"familyTip":"Grow cress or a bean on two saucers: one on the windowsill and one in a cupboard. Check them together each day and let your child decide when to water by touching the soil. After a week, compare: which one is green and strong?","keyIdea":"A plant needs water and light to stay healthy, and it needs to be warm enough. When the soil is dry, the plant droops, so you give it water. Wet soil does not need more: too much water can rot the roots. A plant kept in the dark grows tall, thin and pale, so tall is not the same as healthy. Plants grow toward the light, which is why a plant on a windowsill leans to the window.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What does this plant need? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["💧 Water","☀ Light","✅ It is fine"],"items":[["Droopy leaves, dry soil",0],["Pale leaves, in a cupboard",1],["Green and strong, damp soil",2],["Cracked dry soil",0],["Tall, thin and pale in the dark",1],["Leaves hang down on a hot day",0],["Green leaves on a sunny windowsill",2],["Yellow shoots under a box",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsprout","caption":"Flagship lab: water only when the soil is dry, fix droopy and pale plants, grow a seedling in a cupboard, move a lamp and sort what plants need.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_whbMmX2U8p":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Where does a fish live?","choices":["In water","In a nest","In a burrow"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does a fish need to breathe and swim?","explain":"A fish lives in water. It breathes and swims in it, and can not live out of it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Where does a worm like to live?","choices":["In damp soil","On a hot, dry path","Up in a tree"],"answer":0,"hint":"A worm's skin must stay wet.","explain":"A worm lives in damp soil. The damp keeps its skin wet so it can breathe."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"How does a nest help a baby bird?","choices":["It keeps the eggs warm and safe","It helps the bird swim","It gives the bird a toy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the nest high in the tree and the fox.","explain":"A nest keeps eggs warm, and a high nest keeps them away from foxes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A rabbit sees a fox. What does it do?","choices":["Runs down its burrow to hide","Jumps in the pond","Climbs to a nest"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where is the rabbit's safe home?","explain":"It runs down its burrow. The hole is a safe place to hide."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is the best bug home for minibeasts?","choices":["Logs and dead leaves in a shady spot","A plastic bag in the sun","A bare, dry path"],"answer":0,"hint":"Minibeasts like dark, damp places.","explain":"Logs and leaves in the shade stay dark and damp, so minibeasts move in."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"There are {n} animals in the pond. All are fish but 1 frog. How many fish?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Take the 1 frog away from {n}.","explain":"{n} take away 1 is {=n-1}, so there are {=n-1} fish. Fish and frogs both use the pond.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"You see {n} birds. Each one has its own nest. How many nests?","answer":"n","hint":"One nest for each bird. Count the birds.","explain":"One nest for each bird means {n} nests for {n} birds.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Can a fish live in a nest?","choices":["No, it needs water","Yes, any home will do","Yes, if it is a big nest"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each animal needs a home that suits it.","explain":"No. A fish needs water to breathe. Not any home will do."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Where does a rabbit live?","choices":["In a burrow under the ground","In a pond","In a nest in a tree"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the lab. Where did the rabbit run when the fox came?","explain":"A rabbit digs a burrow. It hides down the hole and keeps safe from foxes."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You lift a log and see {n} woodlice. Then 1 more walks in. How many woodlice now?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Start at {n} and count on 1 more.","explain":"{n} and 1 more is {=n+1}. Woodlice like the dark, damp place under the log.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Why do woodlice live under logs and stones?","choices":["It is dark and damp there","It is hot and dry there","They are lost"],"answer":0,"hint":"Remember the box in the lab. Which side did the woodlice pick?","explain":"Woodlice dry out in the sun. Under a log it is dark and damp, so they stay safe and wet."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A nest has {n} eggs. It is high in a tree, so the fox gets none. How many eggs are still safe?","answer":"n","hint":"The fox can not reach the nest. Did any eggs go?","explain":"None were taken, so all {n} eggs are safe. A high nest keeps eggs out of reach.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Every animal has a home that suits it. We call it a habitat. A fish lives in a pond. It swims and breathes in the water.","A bird makes a nest up in a tree. The nest keeps the eggs warm. It is up high, so a fox can not get them.","A rabbit digs a hole in the ground. It is called a burrow. When a fox comes, the rabbit runs down and hides.","Lift a log. Look under it. It is dark and damp. Woodlice, worms and beetles live there. They dry out in the hot sun, so they hide in the damp."],"adult":["This builds toward the EYFS Early Learning Goal The Natural World: children explore the natural world around them and make observations. It leads into Year 2 Living things and their habitats, where pupils identify that most living things live in habitats to which they are suited, describe how habitats provide for basic needs, and name animals in microhabitats. NGSS K-ESS3-1 asks kindergarteners to model how the needs of animals relate to the places they live.","Key words: habitat (the place where an animal lives), microhabitat (a small habitat, such as under a log or a stone), shelter (a safe place to hide and rest), basic needs (water, food, air and shelter) and suited (the place gives the animal what it needs). Minibeast is a friendly early-years word for small invertebrates such as woodlice, worms, snails and beetles.","Common mistakes: thinking any animal can live anywhere, or that animals choose a home because they like the look of it, rather than because it meets their needs. Children also think all animals live in houses or zoos, or that worms and woodlice prefer sun. Woodlice and worms lose water easily through their bodies, so they seek damp, dark places. Be careful with fish: they need water to breathe through their gills, not just to swim.","In the lab your child carries four animals to their homes, lifts a log and watches woodlice choose the dark, damp side of a box, keeps a blackbird's nest out of a fox's reach, says how each home helps, and builds a bug home that minibeasts move into. The woodlouse result is a model; a real choice chamber shows most, not always all, choosing damp and dark. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"In the park or garden, gently lift a log or a large stone together. Count what you find, ask 'Is it dark and damp under here?', then put the log back exactly as it was so the minibeasts keep their home.","keyIdea":"Animals live in places that suit them. The place an animal lives is its habitat. A fish lives in water, a bird lays eggs in a nest, a rabbit digs a burrow, and a worm lives in damp soil. The home gives the animal what it needs: water, food, or a safe place to hide and keep babies. Small creatures, called minibeasts, like dark, damp spots, such as under a log or a stone.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Wet home or dry home? Drag each animal, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Lives in water","Lives on land"],"items":[["Fish",0],["Rabbit",1],["Frog tadpole",0],["Worm in the soil",1],["Crab in a rock pool",0],["Blackbird",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaghome","caption":"Flagship lab: take animals to their habitats, lift a log and let woodlice choose dark and damp, keep a nest safe from a fox, and build a bug home.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_85mWt1eV2S":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Leo keeps yawning. What does his body need?","choices":["Sleep","Sweets","A run"],"answer":0,"hint":"Yawning is a clue. When do you yawn?","explain":"Yawning means Leo is tired, so his body needs sleep."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What do people and animals need to grow?","choices":["Food, water, sleep and moving","Only food","Only sweets"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of four things. Food is one of them.","explain":"Food, water, sleep and moving all help bodies grow, for people and for pets."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Look at a baby photo of you. What has changed?","choices":["I am bigger now","I am smaller now","Nothing has changed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Were you big or small when you were a baby?","explain":"You were small as a baby. Now you are bigger, and you are still growing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which one makes your heart beat faster?","choices":["Running in the park","Sitting on the sofa","Sleeping"],"answer":0,"hint":"Put your hand on your chest after you run. What do you feel?","explain":"Running makes your heart beat faster, and that makes it strong."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Your mouth is dry after playing. What helps?","choices":["A drink of water","Going to bed","A sweet"],"answer":0,"hint":"A dry mouth is a clue. What does it want?","explain":"A dry mouth means you are thirsty, so a drink of water helps."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"You ran round the tree {n} times and jumped {k} times. How many goes is that?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Count the runs, then count on the jumps.","explain":"{n} runs and {k} jumps make {=n+k} goes. Moving every day makes your muscles strong.","params":{"n":[1,5],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A puppy eats {n} meals each day. How many meals does it eat in 2 days?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Count the meals for one day, then count them again for day two.","explain":"{n} meals each day for 2 days is {=2*n} meals. Animals need food to grow, just like you.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Is staying up very late good for growing?","choices":["No, sleep helps you grow","Yes, more time to play","Yes, if you eat sweets"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does your body do while you sleep?","explain":"Your body grows and rests while you sleep. Staying up late leaves you tired."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Ava's tummy is rumbling. What does her body need?","choices":["Food","Sleep","A ball"],"answer":0,"hint":"A rumbling tummy is a clue. What fills a tummy?","explain":"A rumbling tummy means Ava is hungry, so her body needs food."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Teddy slept {n} hours. He needs 10 hours to fill his sleep battery. How many more hours does he need?","answer":"10-n","hint":"Count on from {n} up to 10.","explain":"From {n} up to 10 is {=10-n} more hours. Children need about 10 to 13 hours of sleep.","params":{"n":[5,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which one is a sometimes food?","choices":["Sweets","An apple","A carrot"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one has lots of sugar?","explain":"Sweets have lots of sugar, so they are a small treat for sometimes. Apples and carrots are good every day."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You had {n} drinks of water today. After running you have 1 more. How many drinks is that?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Start at {n} and count 1 more.","explain":"{n} and 1 more makes {=n+1} drinks. Water helps your body, most of all after you run about.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Look at a baby photo of you. You were small! Now you are big. You are still growing.","To grow, your body needs four things: food, water, sleep and moving. Pets need them too.","Your body tells you what it needs. A rumbling tummy says eat. A dry mouth says drink water. A big yawn says sleep.","Fruit and vegetables are good every day. Sweets are just for sometimes. Sleep all night, and run, jump and climb in the day. That makes your heart strong."],"adult":["In England, Development Matters (2023) asks 3 and 4 year olds to make healthy choices about food, drink, activity and toothbrushing. In Reception, children talk about what keeps them healthy: physical activity, healthy eating, toothbrushing, screen time and a good sleep routine. Year 2 science then covers the basic needs of animals, including humans (water, food and air), and why exercise, the right amounts of different foods and hygiene matter.","The numbers behind the lab: the UK Chief Medical Officers advise that 3 and 4 year olds are active for at least 180 minutes a day, with 60 of them energetic. The American Academy of Sleep Medicine says children aged 3 to 5 need 10 to 13 hours of sleep in 24 hours, naps included. The NHS Eatwell Guide puts fruit and vegetables at the base of every day eating, and foods high in sugar as small and less often.","Common mistakes: thinking food alone makes you grow, so sleep and play do not matter; thinking sweets are good because they give energy; seeing bedtime as a punishment; and thinking grown-ups keep getting taller. Be careful with good food and bad food labels. Every day and sometimes keeps meals calm and is closer to the advice.","In the lab your child drags food, water or a bed to a child whose body gives a clue. They sort foods onto a plate or into a treat jar and drag the moon across the night until a sleep battery fills. They line people up from baby to grown-up, then run, jump and climb while a heart beats faster. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"At bedtime, ask: what did you eat, drink and play today that helps you grow? Look at a baby photo together and find three ways your child has grown.","keyIdea":"People and animals need food, water, sleep and moving to grow and stay healthy. Your body gives clues: a rumbling tummy needs food, a dry mouth needs water and yawning needs sleep. Fruit and vegetables are every day foods; sweets are a sometimes treat. Children need about 10 to 13 hours of sleep a day and lots of active play. You were a baby, and now you are bigger: you are still growing.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Every day food or sometimes food? Drag each one to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Every day","Sometimes"],"items":[["banana",0],["sweets",1],["broccoli",0],["crisps",1],["water",0],["cake",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagthrive","caption":"Flagship lab: answer body clues, sort every day and sometimes foods, fill a sleep battery, line up from baby to grown-up, and run, jump and climb.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_o9sY2RnCE5":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does a seed need to wake up and start growing?","choices":["Water and warmth","Only light","Nothing at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the pot in the cold fridge. Why did it stay asleep?","explain":"A seed needs water and warmth to start. Most seeds can start in the dark, under the soil."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A wet bean sits in a cold fridge for a week. What happens?","choices":["It does not sprout","It sprouts very fast","It turns into a flower"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is a fridge warm or cold?","explain":"The fridge is too cold, so the seed stays asleep. It needs warmth as well as water."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which part grows up to the light?","choices":["The shoot","The root","The seed"],"answer":0,"hint":"One part goes down to drink. The other goes up. Which goes up?","explain":"The shoot grows up to the light and its leaves open at the top. The root grows down."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which comes just after the seed?","choices":["A seed with a root","A big plant","A flower"],"answer":0,"hint":"What came out of the bean in the jar first?","explain":"First the root comes out of the seed. Then the shoot and leaves make a seedling, then a big plant."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Where do new bean seeds come from?","choices":["From a big bean plant","From the soil","From the rain"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the last picture in the life story. What does the big plant make?","explain":"A big bean plant grows pods with new beans inside. Plant a bean and the life story starts again."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"You put {n} beans in a warm pot and {k} beans in a cold fridge. Only the warm ones sprout. How many sprout?","answer":"n","hint":"The cold beans stay asleep. Count only the warm ones.","explain":"Only the {n} warm beans sprout. The {k} cold beans stay asleep, so {=n} beans sprout.","params":{"n":[2,6],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A root is {n} blocks long. The next day it is 1 block longer. How long is it now?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Start at {n} and count 1 more.","explain":"{n} blocks and 1 more is {=n+1} blocks. The root keeps growing down into the soil.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Can a bean start to grow in a dark cupboard if it is wet and warm?","choices":["Yes, it can start in the dark","No, it needs light to start","No, it must stay dry"],"answer":0,"hint":"A seed under the soil is in the dark. Does it still grow?","explain":"Yes. A wet, warm bean starts in the dark. Its leaves come out pale, so it needs light to grow green."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Mo plants a bean in a jar. What comes out of the bean first?","choices":["A white root","A green shoot","A flower"],"answer":0,"hint":"The seed needs a drink before it can grow up. Which part drinks?","explain":"The root comes out first and grows down to drink water. The shoot comes next."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Ella plants {n} seeds. Then she plants 2 more. How many seeds has she planted?","answer":"n+2","hint":"Start at {n} and count on 2 more.","explain":"{n} seeds and 2 more make {=n+2} seeds. Each one needs water and warmth to wake up.","params":{"n":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A bean is planted upside down. Which way does its root grow?","choices":["It turns and grows down","It grows up to the sky","It does not grow"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the bean in the jar. Where did every root go?","explain":"A root always turns to grow down, so an upside down bean still sends its root down into the soil."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A bean shoot has {n} leaves. Then 2 new leaves open. How many leaves now?","answer":"n+2","hint":"Count the leaves you have, then count on 2.","explain":"{n} leaves and 2 new ones make {=n+2} leaves. Leaves need light to grow green.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A seed looks still, but there is a tiny plant inside. It has food packed in, like a lunch box.","Push the seed into the soil and give it water. In a warm room, the seed wakes up.","First a white root pops out. It always grows down, to drink water. Then a green shoot grows up. Leaves open at the top.","Now it is a seedling. With water and light it grows into a big plant. The big plant makes new seeds."],"adult":["In England, Development Matters (2023) asks 3 and 4 year olds to plant seeds and care for growing plants, and to understand the key features of the life cycle of a plant. Year 1 science names the basic structure of a plant (roots, stem, leaves, flowers), and Year 2 asks children to observe and describe how seeds and bulbs grow into mature plants, and how plants need water, light and a suitable temperature.","The words to use: the seed germinates when it takes in water and is warm enough. The first root (the radicle) always comes out first and turns to grow down, because roots respond to gravity. The shoot grows up. A young plant with its first leaves is a seedling. A broad bean or runner bean in a clear jar, pressed against wet kitchen paper, shows the root in about 2 to 5 days in a warm room.","Common mistakes: thinking the shoot comes first; thinking a seed needs light to start (beans, peas and sunflowers germinate in the dark under the soil, though a few tiny seeds such as lettuce do need light); thinking the seed eats soil (it lives on its own food store until the leaves can make food); and thinking an upside down seed will grow the wrong way. Be careful: a seed in a cold fridge stays asleep, so warmth matters as much as water.","In the lab your child drags a seed into a pot and waters it, slides the days along to watch a bean grow in a glass jar (lying the right way, on its side and upside down), carries a wet pot to a sunny window, a dark cupboard and a cold fridge, puts root, shoot and leaf labels on a seedling, and lines up the life story. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"Push a bean between wet kitchen paper and the side of a clear jar. Keep the paper damp and check each morning. Ask: which came out first, the root or the shoot? Which way is the root growing?","keyIdea":"A seed is a baby plant with a packed lunch inside. Plant it in soil and give it water. In a warm place it wakes up: this is called germination. The root comes out first and grows down, even if the seed is upside down. Next the shoot grows up, and leaves open at the top. Seed, root, seedling, big plant: the big plant makes new seeds.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Root or shoot? Drag each card to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Root","Shoot"],"items":[["grows down",0],["grows up",1],["under the soil",0],["green",1],["comes out first",0],["has leaves at the top",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagseed","caption":"Flagship lab: plant and water a seed, watch a bean's root and shoot grow in a jar, find what wakes a seed, label root, shoot and leaf, and order the life story.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_13CtLTcWUB":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A zoo has {a} lions and {b} tigers. Lions and tigers eat only meat. How many carnivores is that?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Lions and tigers are both carnivores. Add them up.","explain":"{a} lions + {b} tigers = {=a+b} carnivores, because they all eat only meat.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A hen pecks at seeds and {n} worms. What is a hen?","choices":["An omnivore","A herbivore","A carnivore"],"answer":0,"hint":"Seeds come from plants. Worms are animals.","explain":"A hen eats seeds, which are plants, and worms, which are animals. Eating both makes it an omnivore.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What does a herbivore eat?","choices":["Only plants","Only meat","Plants and meat"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the rabbit in the lab. Which foods did it take?","explain":"A herbivore eats only plants, such as grass, leaves and fruit. Cows and rabbits are herbivores."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"An elephant is the biggest animal on land. Which group is it in?","choices":["Herbivore","Carnivore","Omnivore"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do not go by size. What does an elephant eat?","explain":"An elephant eats grass, leaves, bark and fruit, all plants, so it is a herbivore. Big does not mean meat eater."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"An owl catches mice and beetles to eat. What is an owl?","choices":["A carnivore","A herbivore","An omnivore"],"answer":0,"hint":"Mice and beetles are animals. Does the owl eat plants too?","explain":"Mice and beetles are animals, so an owl eats meat. It does not eat plants, so it is a carnivore."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is the best reason a cow is a herbivore?","choices":["It eats only grass and plants","It lives on a farm","It has spots"],"answer":0,"hint":"The group is about food. Which reason talks about food?","explain":"A cow is a herbivore because it eats only plants. Where it lives and how it looks are not reasons."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"You eat toast, apples and fish fingers. What does that make you?","choices":["An omnivore","A herbivore","A carnivore"],"answer":0,"hint":"Toast and apples come from plants. Fish is meat.","explain":"You eat plants and meat, so you are an omnivore, just like a bear or a pig."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A bear eats berries and fish. Sam says it is a carnivore. What did Sam forget?","choices":["Berries are plants, so it eats both","Bears do not eat fish","Nothing, Sam is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at both foods. Is one of them a plant?","explain":"Sam forgot the berries. A bear eats plants and meat, so it is an omnivore, not a carnivore."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A giraffe eats {n} kilograms of leaves a day and no meat at all. What is a giraffe?","choices":["A herbivore","A carnivore","An omnivore"],"answer":0,"hint":"Leaves come from plants. Does it eat any meat?","explain":"A giraffe eats only plants, such as leaves, so it is a herbivore. Being tall and big does not change that.","params":{"n":[20,30]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"At the farm there are {a} cows and {b} sheep. Cows and sheep both eat only plants. How many herbivores are there?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Every cow and every sheep is a herbivore. Add the two numbers.","explain":"{a} cows + {b} sheep = {=a+b} herbivores, because they all eat only plants.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A pig eats apples, corn and worms. Which group is it in?","choices":["Omnivore","Herbivore","Carnivore"],"answer":0,"hint":"Apples and corn are plants. Worms are animals.","explain":"A pig eats plants and animals, so it is an omnivore. Worms count as meat."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Max says a cat is a herbivore because it is small and soft. Is he right?","choices":["No, a cat eats meat, so it is a carnivore","Yes, small animals eat plants","Yes, soft animals eat plants"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does a cat eat? Being small or soft is not about food.","explain":"No. A cat eats meat and fish, so it is a carnivore. How an animal looks does not tell us its group."}],"explain":{"kid":["Every animal needs food. But they do not all eat the same food. Some eat plants. Some eat meat. Some eat both.","A cow eats grass. A rabbit eats grass and leaves. They eat only plants. An animal that eats only plants is a herbivore.","A lion eats meat. A cat eats meat and fish. An animal that eats only meat is a carnivore. A bear eats berries and fish. It eats plants and meat, so it is an omnivore. You are an omnivore too!","Big does not mean meat. An elephant is huge, and it eats only plants. So to sort an animal, ask: what does it eat?"],"adult":["In England, the Year 1 programme of study for Animals, including humans asks pupils to identify and name a variety of common animals that are carnivores, herbivores and omnivores. The idea links to Year 2 work on how animals get food and to Year 4 work on teeth and food chains.","The three terms are about diet. Herbivores eat plants, carnivores eat other animals, and omnivores eat both. Fish counts as meat here, and so do insects and worms, which is why a hen that eats seeds and worms is an omnivore. As a species, humans are omnivores, although some people choose not to eat meat.","Common mistakes: thinking big or fierce-looking animals must be carnivores, when elephants, rhinos and gorillas eat mostly plants; thinking small or cute animals must eat plants, when a pet cat needs meat; putting a bear or a pig in a one-food group; and sorting by habitat or looks, such as farm animals or furry animals, instead of food. Ask your child for the reason every time.","In the lab your child drags food to a rabbit, a cat and a bear and sees which foods each one takes. Then they sort six animals into a plants bowl, a meat bowl and a both bowl, and pick the reason for three groups, where only a food reason is right."]},"familyTip":"At dinner, sort what is on your plates into foods from plants and foods from animals. Then ask: if a rabbit sat down with us, what could it eat? What about a cat? And a bear? Next time you see an animal at the park or on TV, ask your child what it eats, and whether that makes it a herbivore, a carnivore or an omnivore.","keyIdea":"We can sort animals by what they eat. A herbivore eats only plants, like a cow or a rabbit. A carnivore eats only meat, like a lion or a cat. An omnivore eats both plants and meat, like a bear, a pig or a person. What an animal eats decides its group, not how big it is, how it looks or where it lives.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does this animal eat only plants, only meat, or both? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Herbivore","Carnivore","Omnivore"],"items":[["horse",0],["tiger",1],["pig",2],["giraffe",0],["wolf",1],["bear",2],["sheep",0],["owl",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdiet","caption":"Flagship lab: feed a rabbit, a cat and a bear by dragging food, sort six animals into plant, meat and both bowls, and pick the food reason for each group.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_xT6jPzyj92":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A tree has {a} big branches. Each branch has 2 leaves. How many leaves is that?","answer":"a*2","hint":"Count in twos, one 2 for every branch: 2, 4, 6 and so on.","explain":"{a} branches with 2 leaves each makes {=a*2} leaves. The branches hold the leaves up to the sun.","params":{"a":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"There are {a} plants in a pot. Each plant has one stem. How many stems are there?","answer":"a","hint":"Each plant has just one stem, so count one stem for each plant.","explain":"One stem for each plant, so {a} plants have {a} stems. Each stem holds its plant up.","params":{"a":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which part catches sunlight to make food for the plant?","choices":["The roots","The leaves","The seeds"],"answer":1,"hint":"This part is often flat and green, so it can catch lots of light.","explain":"Leaves catch sunlight. With light, air and water, they make the plant's food."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Mia says, 'A tree is not a plant. It is too big and made of wood.' Is she right?","choices":["Yes, trees are not plants","No, a tree has roots, a trunk and leaves, so it is a plant","No, a tree is an animal"],"answer":1,"hint":"Think about the parts a tree has. Does it have roots and leaves like other plants?","explain":"A tree is a plant. Its trunk is a big, woody stem, and it has roots and leaves like other plants."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"We eat a carrot. Which part of the plant is it?","choices":["A fruit","A leaf","A root"],"answer":2,"hint":"Where does a carrot grow: up in the air or down in the soil?","explain":"A carrot grows down in the soil, so it is a root. A fruit holds seeds, and a carrot has none inside."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Sam says plants eat soil to get their food. What is true?","choices":["Plants make their own food in their leaves","Plants eat soil with their roots","Plants get food from their flowers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Roots take in water. Which part uses sunlight?","explain":"Plants make their own food in their leaves, using light, air and water. Roots take in water, not food."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"You put a white flower in blue water. Which way does the blue water go?","choices":["Up the stem to the petals","Down from the petals to the stem","It stays in the glass"],"answer":0,"hint":"Water goes in at the bottom of the stem and climbs up like a straw.","explain":"The water goes up the stem to the petals, so the white petals turn blue. Stems carry water up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which part of a tree is its stem?","choices":["The trunk","The leaves","The roots"],"answer":0,"hint":"This part goes straight up from the ground and holds the tree up.","explain":"The trunk is the tree's stem. It is thick and woody, holds the tree up and carries water to the branches."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A flower has {a} petals. One petal falls off. How many petals are left on the flower?","answer":"a-1","hint":"Start at {a} and count back one. The petals are part of the flower.","explain":"{a} take away 1 is {=a-1}, so {=a-1} petals are left. Petals help bring in bees so the flower can make seeds.","params":{"a":[4,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A plant has {a} leaves on one side of the stem and {b} on the other. How many leaves catch the sun?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Count the leaves on both sides of the stem and add them together.","explain":"{a} and {b} make {=a+b}. All {=a+b} leaves catch sunlight to make food for the plant.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which part of a plant grows under the soil?","choices":["The flower","The roots","The leaves"],"answer":1,"hint":"This part holds the plant in place and drinks up water.","explain":"Roots grow down into the soil. They hold the plant still and take in water."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"What is the job of a flower?","choices":["To make seeds","To hold the plant up","To drink water from the soil"],"answer":0,"hint":"New plants grow from something small that the flower makes.","explain":"Flowers make seeds, and seeds grow into new plants. The stem holds the plant up and the roots take in water."}],"explain":{"kid":["Look at a plant. Under the soil are the roots. They hold on tight so the plant does not fall over. They drink up water too. The stem grows up out of the soil. It holds the plant up tall. Water goes up the stem like a straw.","The leaves are flat and green. They catch the sun. With sun, air and water, a plant makes its own food. It does not eat soil.","The flower is at the top. It can be bright to bring in bees. The flower makes seeds. Seeds grow into new plants.","A tree is a plant too. It has roots. Its stem is the big trunk, made of wood. Its branches hold the leaves."],"adult":["This is the Year 1 Plants unit of the English national curriculum, which asks pupils to 'identify and name a variety of common wild and garden plants, including deciduous and evergreen trees' and to 'identify and describe the basic structure of a variety of common flowering plants, including trees'. The four named parts are roots, stem (trunk), leaves and flowers. In the US, NGSS 1-LS1-1 covers how plant parts such as roots, stems, leaves, flowers and fruits help a plant survive and grow.","Each part has a job. Roots anchor the plant and absorb water and minerals. The stem supports the plant and carries water up from the roots and food made in the leaves to the rest of the plant. Leaves use light, air and water to make food (the word photosynthesis comes later, in Year 3). Flowers are where seeds are made, often helped by insects carrying pollen. A tree has the same plan: the trunk is a woody stem, and branches are side stems.","Common mistakes to watch for: thinking plants take their food from the soil (they take in water and minerals, but make food in their leaves); saying a tree is not a plant because it is big and woody; thinking flowers are only for decoration; thinking water goes in through the leaves; and calling a carrot a fruit or a stem when it is a root. Celery is a tricky one: the crunchy stalk we eat is a leaf stalk, so this lesson keeps to clearer examples.","In the lab your child builds a plant by putting each label in its place, matches each part to its job, follows blue water from the roots up to a white flower that turns blue, labels a tree, and sorts foods by the part of the plant we eat."]},"familyTip":"Put a white flower or a pale lettuce leaf in a glass of water with a few drops of blue food colouring. Check it the next day: the colour has climbed up the stem and into the petals or leaf. Ask your child which way the water went and which part it went in through.","keyIdea":"Most plants we see have the same four main parts. Roots grow down in the soil: they hold the plant in place and take in water. The stem holds the plant up and carries water to the leaves. Leaves catch sunlight so the plant can make its own food. Flowers make seeds that grow into new plants. A tree is a plant too: its trunk is a thick, woody stem, and its branches carry the leaves.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which part does this job? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Roots","Stem","Leaves","Flower"],"items":[["Hold on in the soil",0],["Take in water",0],["Hold the plant up",1],["Carry water up",1],["Catch sunlight",2],["Make food",2],["Make seeds",3],["Bring in bees",3]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagplant","caption":"Flagship lab: build a plant, match each part to its job, follow the water up a stem, label a tree and sort foods by the part we eat.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_fDoE-pL6Jv":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"At the farm there are {a} cows and {b} hens. How many of them are mammals?","answer":"a","hint":"Cows have hair and feed their calves milk. Hens have feathers.","explain":"The {a} cows are mammals. The {b} hens have feathers, so they are birds. The answer is {a}.","params":{"a":[2,7],"b":[3,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A zoo has {a} snakes, {b} crocodiles and 4 penguins. How many reptiles are there?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Reptiles have dry, scaly skin. Penguins have feathers.","explain":"Snakes and crocodiles are reptiles: {a} + {b} = {=a+b}. Penguins are birds, so they do not count.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What covers a frog?","choices":["Smooth, damp skin","Dry scales","Feathers"],"answer":0,"hint":"A frog is an amphibian. Think of how it feels.","explain":"A frog has smooth, damp skin. That is what makes it an amphibian. Dry scales are for reptiles."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Mo says a whale is a fish because it lives in the sea. What is true?","choices":["A whale is a mammal: it breathes air and its babies drink milk","Mo is right, it is a fish","A whale is an amphibian"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it where the animal lives, or its body, that tells you the group?","explain":"A whale breathes air and feeds its babies milk, so it is a mammal. Living in the sea does not make an animal a fish."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which one is the odd one out? Eagle, bat, penguin.","choices":["Bat","Eagle","Penguin"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two have feathers. One has fur.","explain":"The bat has fur, so it is a mammal. The eagle and the penguin have feathers: they are birds."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which animal is a reptile?","choices":["Tortoise","Toad","Trout"],"answer":0,"hint":"Reptiles have dry, scaly skin.","explain":"A tortoise has dry scales, so it is a reptile. A toad is an amphibian and a trout is a fish."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Can every bird fly?","choices":["No, a penguin is a bird but it cannot fly","Yes, all birds can fly","No, birds that cannot fly are fish"],"answer":0,"hint":"What makes a bird a bird: flying, or feathers and a beak?","explain":"A penguin cannot fly, but it has feathers and a beak, so it is still a bird."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"In a field there are {a} rabbits and {b} crows. How many of them are birds?","answer":"b","hint":"Rabbits have fur. Crows have feathers and a beak. Which ones are birds?","explain":"The {b} crows have feathers, so they are birds. The {a} rabbits have fur: they are mammals. The answer is {b}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[3,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A pond has {a} frogs and {b} fish. How many of them are amphibians?","answer":"a","hint":"Frogs have smooth, damp skin. Fish have fins and gills. Which ones are amphibians?","explain":"Only the frogs are amphibians, so the answer is {a}. The {b} fish are in the fish group.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A bat can fly. Which group is it in?","choices":["Mammal","Bird","Reptile"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does a bat have fur or feathers?","explain":"A bat has fur and feeds its babies milk, so it is a mammal. Flying does not make an animal a bird."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which clue tells a shark from a dolphin?","choices":["Dolphin babies drink milk","They both have fins","They both live in the sea"],"answer":0,"hint":"A good clue is true for one animal and not the other.","explain":"Dolphin babies drink milk, so a dolphin is a mammal. A shark is a fish. Fins and the sea are true of both."}],"explain":{"kid":["Look at what covers an animal. A bear has fur. An owl has feathers. A fish has scales. A snake has scales too, but they are dry. A frog has smooth, damp skin.","We sort animals into groups by their bodies. Mammals have fur or hair, and their babies drink milk. Birds have feathers, a beak and wings. Fish have fins and gills. Reptiles have dry scales. Amphibians have smooth, damp skin.","Some animals are tricky. A whale lives in the sea, but it is not a fish. It breathes air and its babies drink milk, so it is a mammal. A bat can fly, but it has fur. It is a mammal too.","A penguin cannot fly, but it has feathers and a beak. So it is a bird. Where an animal lives does not tell you its group. Its body does."],"adult":["The English national curriculum asks Year 1 pupils to 'identify and name a variety of common animals including fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals' and to 'describe and compare the structure of a variety of common animals'. In the US, NGSS 1-LS1-1 asks children to use what they notice about the external parts of animals, such as fins, feathers and shells. These five groups are all vertebrates, animals with a backbone.","Each group is defined by its body. Mammals have hair or fur and feed their young on milk. Birds have feathers, a beak, two wings and two legs, and lay hard-shelled eggs. Fish live in water, breathe with gills and have fins, and most are covered in scales. Reptiles have dry, scaly skin and breathe air. Amphibians, such as frogs, toads and newts, have smooth, damp skin and usually begin life in water as tadpoles.","Common mistakes: sorting by habitat or movement, so a whale or a dolphin becomes a fish and a bat becomes a bird; thinking all birds fly, then rejecting the penguin or the ostrich; muddling reptiles and amphibians, because a lizard and a newt look alike; thinking a snake is slimy when its scales are dry; and calling every small creature an animal group of its own. Ask 'what covers its body?' and 'how do its babies feed?' rather than 'where does it live?'.","In the lab your child matches animals to their coverings, sorts eight animals (including the whale, the bat and the penguin) into the five groups, builds a bird, a fish and a mammal from body parts, finds the odd one out, and chooses the clue that tells a shark from a dolphin."]},"familyTip":"On a walk or with a picture book, play 'fur, feathers, scales or skin?'. Point at an animal and ask what covers it, then which group that puts it in. Try a tricky one: a duck swims, but is it a fish?","keyIdea":"Animals with backbones are sorted into five groups by their bodies, not by where they live. Mammals have fur or hair and feed their babies milk. Birds have feathers, a beak and wings. Fish have fins and gills, and most have scales. Reptiles have dry, scaly skin. Amphibians, like frogs, have smooth, damp skin.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which group is each animal in? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Mammal","Bird","Reptile","Amphibian"],"items":[["Cat: fur",0],["Whale: babies drink milk",0],["Robin: feathers",1],["Penguin: feathers and a beak",1],["Snake: dry scales",2],["Tortoise: dry scales",2],["Toad: smooth, damp skin",3],["Newt: smooth, damp skin",3]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcoat","caption":"Flagship lab: match coverings, sort animals into five groups, build an animal, find the odd one out, tell look-alikes apart.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
